{
 "what": "ANIMATION [the PC playtest: the bodies' part] (rule 164 items 21, 22, 31; Paolo 10/24: 'if they're using pistols they shouldn't look like they're holding rifles'; 'when they're in a building they get extra cover stats... they shouldn't be standing up, they should look like they're in cover'; 'more and better animations when people hold things'). THE SOURCE OF THE HELD CLIPS (rule 128, the kept scene): every clip is a list of HELD keys (three a beat at 120, Crashbox stop-motion holds, rule 115(d)); every key is a list of STEPS run in order on the rig's man (the skeleton every person in the game is built on): 'ops' turns bones about the body's own axes (F forward, U up, L his left; degrees by the right hand) and 'MOVE's a bone (model units, the skeleton's own, f u l); 'reach' puts a wrist on a point by a two-bone reach (the frame 'body' is the armature's axes from the named bone's head as it stands at that step, so an aimed arm points where he faces whatever the chest does); 'legs' plants both feet where they stood ('plant') or KNEELS (the named knee on the ground and its shin back along it, the other foot a step forward, the pelvis dropped exactly so the knee touches: 'seat' sits him back toward his heel, the resting kneel behind cover; without it the high kneel he rises to to fire over it). The tool is tools/bohemia_animation_what_a_man_holds.py.",
